{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.repl.dao.space-index.md | sed -n '1,105p;185,225p'\nrg -n -C 9 'repl-state|token.independen|summary|index-publication' src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/yin.repl.md\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# yin.repl: automatic code indexing\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design seam; the owner has ruled automatic indexing on evaluation.\n     4\tThe `q` surface is user-require by owner ruling (2026-09-27).\n     5\t\n     6\t## Composition\n     7\t\n     8\t`yin.repl/make-session` creates `program-out` and keeps its writer as\n     9\t`:row-stream` and the evaluator's independent reader as `:row-observer`\n    10\t(`src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:523-554`). The expander appends one expanded\n    11\t`[root rows]` packet there for each forwarded program\n    12\t(`yin.vm.macro.md`, section 2.4); `run-evaluator-stage` reads it at\n    13\t`src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:767-779`. Attach a second, independently\n    14\tadvanced observer to this same medium when building the session. After\n    15\t`run-expander-stage` forwards a packet, advance the index observer for\n    16\tthat packet as part of the evaluation round. Do not put indexing in a\n    17\tloader, runner, frontend, or the link pair. The link pair serves content\n    18\tfor `require`; it is not the evaluated-program stream.\n    19\t\n    20\tThe observer consumes packets whether the VM later returns, parks, or\n    21\traises. A failed expansion forwards no packet, so it contributes no\n    22\texpanded code. Give the index reader the same gap and reset discipline as\n    23\tthe evaluator reader: a lost packet must be reported, not called indexed.\n    24\tReset and VM selection rebuild the session and its observer together.\n    25\t\n    26\t## Indexed facts\n    27\t\n    28\tThe packet's root is the program's content address; each canonical flat\n    29\trow `[id tag & slots]` has its own content-addressed node id\n    30\t(`yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`, sections 4 and 6.1). Maintain the `$ast`\n    31\trow relation and its derived occurrence relation from these rows. This\n    32\tis the direct input used by `dao.space.query/q` in the free-names tests\n    33\t(`test/dao/space/query_test.cljc:1121-1161`). Do not relabel these\n    34\tvariable-arity rows as five-slot datoms.\n    35\t\n    36\tFor the `dao.space.index` transactor's covered indexes, project the same\n    37\texpanded tree to local `[e a v t m]` AST facts as specified in\n    38\t`yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`, section 6.5. Its `e` is a transactor-local\n    39\tentity id, with `:yin/address` pointing back to the node address; it is\n    40\tnot the node identity. Commit a program's facts as one atomic transaction\n    41\trecord, `{:dao.space/transaction {:t n :datoms [...]}}`. The transactor\n    42\tallocates `t`; the shell never supplies a clock. Attribute queries and\n    43\t`pull` use this projection; positional structural queries use `$ast`.\n    44\t\n    45\tThe session that minted the evaluation is provenance. Record its stable\n    46\tshell token as a fact on a metadata entity and refer to that entity in\n    47\tthe datoms' `m` slot, following the metadata-entity convention; `m` is a\n    48\tlocal integer, not a session string. Preserve the root/round association\n    49\toutside the content-hashed rows. Index code and its claims only: printed\n    50\toutput, last-value history, and ephemeral evaluation results do not enter\n    51\tthe code index by default.\n    52\t\n    53\t**Rows every round** (owner decision of 2026-10-01,\n    54\t`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 5.1). After a program's transaction commits, the\n    55\tindexer materializes every row of its expanded tree into the index store\n    56\t(`yin.vm/materialize-tree!`), before the round publishes, so a\n    57\t`:yin/address` fact names content the store holds and a peer can load a\n    58\tprogram's tree by its `:yin.repl/root` address. A row put that fails is\n    59\tthe round's failure at stage `:materialize`, and the round does not\n    60\tpublish. The committed program's row set stays unwritten: every later\n    61\tround that commits (and every name transaction) writes the unwritten rows\n    62\tfirst, oldest first, and publishes only when none remain, so HEAD never\n    63\tmoves over a missing row. When they are all written the failure is\n    64\tcleared, the publication covers every committed transaction, and the\n    65\tround says the index caught up. Restored history is not re-materialized.\n    66\t\n    67\t**Names are claims in the same index** (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 6.1).\n    68\t`(yin.link/publish 'm '[exports])` commits the signed name envelopes as\n    69\tone transaction — one entity per envelope carrying\n    70\t`:yin.module/envelope` and `:yin.module/proof`, the session's metadata\n    71\tentity (no program root) in `m` — and publishes at once, so the round's\n    72\tHEAD write announces the module's blobs with the index naming them.\n    73\t\n    74\t## Query surface: owner ruling (2026-09-27)\n    75\t\n    76\t**User require.** `dao.space.query/q` is not bound in the session; the\n    77\tuser's own `(require 'dao.space.query)` binds it, running over the\n    78\tindex this observer maintains. Ruled for `yin.repl`'s explicit, minimal\n    79\tshell contract. Binding `q` at the first prompt (the IDE posture) was\n    80\tconsidered and is a composition a host can still make; the shell does\n    81\tnot. Automatic indexing is a session composition\n    82\tpolicy; exposing an extra language binding is a separate policy. The\n    83\towner can choose the first-prompt posture without changing the medium,\n    84\ttransaction format, or index observer.\n    85\t\n    86\t## Cost and boundary\n    87\t\n    88\tStartup adds one observer attachment and a local index/transactor value;\n    89\tthere is no eager scan of program history or remote fetch. An idle\n    90\tsession performs no indexing work. Each forwarded evaluation pays a\n    91\tlinear pass over its expanded rows, one atomic local transaction for the\n    92\tdatom projection, and the index update; `q` pays its own query cost when\n    93\tcalled. This work is bounded by that program's size and is acceptable as\n    94\tthe cost of making every evaluated program queryable. Publication of\n    95\tcovered indexes can follow the index's normal cadence, outside the\n    96\tevaluation critical path.\n    97\t\n    98\tThis is neither ShiBi authorization nor the `dao.jing.content` service\n    99\tin `dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md`, section 1. It adds no\n   100\twire shape. It composes beside that epic's middleware, remote channel,\n   101\tcontent service, REPL service, UDP, pair, and UCF slices; their transport\n   102\tand content-service file sets need no change for this local observer.\n   103\t\n   104\t## Durable store: startup, HEAD, and the directory lock\n   105\t\n   185\tOn open, an absent HEAD means an empty index. A malformed HEAD, a\n   186\tmissing or invalid manifest, or an unreadable index node — the full\n   187\t`read-manifest` traversal over the opened store, walking every index\n   188\troot the manifest names (EAVT, AEVT, AVET, VAET), each of which must\n   189\tcover exactly the manifest's `:count` datoms — refuses startup rather\n   190\tthan starting empty. An unreferenced blob after a\n   191\tcrash is harmless, and a crash before the rename keeps the previous\n   192\tpublished snapshot: both are HEAD naming an older valid manifest, which\n   193\topens and recovers.\n   194\t\n   195\tWhat the open recovered — `{:manifest <address or nil> :datoms <the\n   196\twalked snapshot or nil>}` — is `:recovery` on the store handle and\n   197\t`:index-recovery` on the shell state, and the shell installs it before\n   198\tit admits any evaluation (`yin.repl.index/rehydrate`). The indexer's\n   199\tlog becomes a fresh complete-retention memory log holding the recovered\n   200\tdatoms as transaction records, one per original `t` in ascending order,\n   201\teach datom and its `t` preserved, so the round's transactor keeps\n   202\tderiving the next `t` from it. Entity allocation resumes one past the\n   203\tgreatest restored entity or metadata id (never below\n   204\t`datom/first-user-id`), so restored and new ids never collide. The\n   205\trecovered manifest is the indexer's published one and its transaction\n   206\tcounts are the restored ones, so `q` answers the previous run's facts\n   207\tbefore any new evaluation, and the first publication after a restart\n   208\tcovers old and new facts — HEAD moves to a manifest holding both.\n   209\t\n   210\tEvery process start mints a new shell token. Restored facts keep their\n   211\toriginal session tokens and root and round metadata, since they are the\n   212\tdatoms as committed; new facts carry the new token, so `q` over the\n   213\thistory view tells the runs apart.\n   214\t\n   215\tWith a durable store, `(reset)` and VM selection rebuild the VM, the\n   216\texpander, and their observers, but the new indexer continues the old\n   217\tone's log, entity allocation, counts, and published manifest\n   218\t(`yin.repl.index/carry-over`): they never reset `t` or entity\n   219\tallocation, and the published index stays queryable once\n   220\t`dao.space.query` is required again. With the default memory store they\n   221\tstart an empty index, exactly as before.\n   222\t\n   223\t## DHT store: `dht:<dir>` (DHT epic S5)\n   224\t\n   225\t`--index-store dht:<dir>` (or `{:type :dht :dir dir ...}` as\n191-is fatal to the current evaluation:\n192-the loss is reported and the shell refuses further evaluation until `(reset)`,\n193-rather than resuming as if execution were complete.\n194-\n195-**The evaluator is step-driven everywhere.** One `repl-step` owns all REPL and\n196-RPC state on every host; input adapters only append lines to the composition's\n197-input medium. A remote evaluation returns immediately with a request id, and\n198-the result prints when it completes. Input typed while a request is\n199-outstanding is queued, not evaluated; local control commands (`disconnect`,\n200:`quit`, `help`, `repl-state`) bypass that queue so a slow remote cannot trap\n201-the operator.\n202-\n203-**A killed connection is reported, never timed out.** Losing the connection\n204-detaches every outstanding request: the driver prints\n205-`;; remote request N lost: …` rather than holding the request against a\n206-deadline, because the layer has no clock. The served stream is untouched by a\n207-detach; typing `(connect …)` with the same URL reattaches through the same\n208-client medium — the deposit medium and its cursor survive the socket's death,\n209-and only the attachment id changes.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '309|failure|manifest-address|VM|uuid|session|findings|Completed' collab/jolt/1794036904000-orchestrator-stream-eval-manifest-diagnosis.muse-wonder-1.0.stdout.log | tail -70\"","aggregated_output":"3:muse: warning: rules file at /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/CLAUDE.md is ignored this session because AGENTS.md takes precedence in that directory; merge still-applicable guidance into AGENTS.md or remove one of the two files\n7:Completed-GMT: 2026-11-07 07:39:37 GMT\n8:Completed-Local: 2026-11-07 14:39:37 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n10:## Diagnosis (deliverable 1): the provenance session token hashed into every index leaf\n12:**Exact locus.** The divergent manifest field is all four `:indexes` root addresses (`:eavt`/`:aevt`/`:avet`/`:vaet`); `:count` and `:branching-factor` (512) agree. One datom per transaction differs: `[meta-e :yin.repl/session <shell-token> t m]` minted in [packet->tx-data](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:116). The token is `(str (random-uuid))` per shell in [create-state](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:926), passed as `:session-token` in [make-session](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:790). Each test session (`session` in [stream_eval_test.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/clojure/stream_eval_test.cljc:31)) therefore indexes the same 15 programs with a different token string.\n16:**Ruled out.** CBOR sorts map keys/set elements by canonical bytes ([cbor.cljc:794](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:794)); bulk build sorts before shaping ([from-sequential](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc:2059)); frontend temporaries are positional `dsg__` names (\"lowering is deterministic,\" [yang/clojure.cljc:167](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:167)); row ids are content keys ([vm.cljc:1155](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:1155)). Stream identities and the expander token ([repl.cljc:738](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:738)) appear only in cursors and the dropped macro log packet, never in tree rows; the test's `strip` already normalizes the cursor UUID. The token is intentional provenance (\"Record its stable shell token as a fact,\" [yin.repl.dao.space-index.md:45](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.repl.dao.space-index.md:45)), and the equivalence test holds the token fixed (`\"equivalence\"`, [index_test.cljc:319](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/repl/index_test.cljc:319)) — equivalence was only ever proven per-token. Regression window: `:index-publication` (with `:manifest-address`) entered `repl-state` in `3c301b65`; before that, summaries carried no address and parity held.\n29:Attempted three times from this session; every attempt failed before connecting with `No user exists for uid 501` (sandbox blocks the `getpwuid` lookup `ssh` requires; unsandboxed retry is denied under `--approval-mode never`). No slow test was run locally (owner rule). Existing captured evidence stands unreproduced by me: nightly `20261106-0944` hashes `blake3-4be79d80…/d034de03…/96539504…/00f3050a…` (relay) and `290876bb/7e57be75/7043f3a6/139678fe` vs JVM `6434f85a/6dcec888/077211ce/972ce5d2` (findings doc) — same 15 transactions, only `:manifest-address` divergent. On shiva, confirm by printing each VM's manifest and diffing `read-datoms` output: the sole differing datoms will be the 15 `:yin.repl/session` values (scratch-only edit, never committed).\n33:In [repl.cljc:2209](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:2209), drop `:manifest-address` from `:index-publication` (keep `:cursor :transactions`); update the pinning assertion at [index_test.cljc:288](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/repl/index_test.cljc:288) to match. Rationale: the manifest address correctly hashes provenance-bearing con