{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/yin.repl.md | sed -n '1,190p'\nrg -n 'manifest-address' test/ | tail -35\nrg -n 'repl-state|serializable summary|token.independen' docs/design src/cljc/yin/vm/docs | head -65\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# yin.repl: the Interface to datom.world\n     2\t\n     3\t`yin.repl` is the primary user interface into datom.world. It started as a\n     4\tREPL and still opens with a prompt, but it is more than a REPL: one program\n     5\tthat is a shell evaluating code on `yin.vm`, an endpoint (`serve!` answers\n     6\tother shells, `connect` reaches a remote one over WebSockets), and a node\n     7\tthat publishes code by signed name and loads other nodes' code over the\n     8\tDHT. It runs on `yin.vm`, requires no legacy namespaces, and runs on the\n     9\tJVM, Node and Dart.\n    10\t\n    11\t## Starting and connecting\n    12\t\n    13\t`(host)` prints the runtime the shell runs on (`Host: JVM 21.0.5`, `Host: Node v25.6.1`, `Host: Dart`), which tells attached REPLs apart over `(connect)`.\n    14\t\n    15\tThe entry points are the REPL aliases:\n    16\t\n    17\t```bash\n    18\tclj -M:clj-yin-repl --port 8080 --headless\n    19\t```\n    20\t\n    21\tFor ClojureDart (`cljd`), use the `cljd-yin-repl-build` alias to compile the Dart source, and then run the generated executable natively via `dart run`:\n    22\t\n    23\t```bash\n    24\tclj -M:clojuredart:cljd-yin-repl-build compile\n    25\tdart run bin/yin_repl_main.dart --port 8080 --headless\n    26\t```\n    27\t\n    28\tFor ClojureScript (`cljs` on Node), you can run it directly using the shadow-cljs alias:\n    29\t\n    30\t```bash\n    31\tclj -M:cljs-yin-repl --port 8080 --headless\n    32\t```\n    33\t\n    34\tOr you can compile it to a standalone Node script:\n    35\t\n    36\t```bash\n    37\tnpx shadow-cljs release yin-repl\n    38\tnode target/yin-repl.js --port 8080 --headless\n    39\t```\n    40\t\n    41\t```clojure\n    42\tyin> (connect \"daostream:ws://localhost:8080/repl\")\n    43\tAttaching to daostream:ws://localhost:8080/repl\n    44\tConnected to daostream:ws://localhost:8080/repl\n    45\t```\n    46\t\n    47\tAn absent URL path means `/repl`; an explicit `/` remains `/`.\n    48\t\n    49\tThe wire codec defaults to CBOR. `--codec <cbor|transit>` sets the wire profile\n    50\tof the served shell (when `--port` is used); at the prompt,\n    51\t`(connect url :codec :transit)` or `:cbor` sets the client connection's profile.\n    52\tBoth ends of a connection must use the same codec.\n    53\t\n    54\t## Using Python at the prompt\n    55\t\n    56\tThe REPL starts a frontend catalog with three frontends installed: `:yang/clojure`\n    57\t(the default), `:yang/php`, and `:yang/python` — implemented by the ANTLR\n    58\tmulti-target parser (revision f4), which parses Python source with the\n    59\tvendored Python 3 grammar and lowers it through the yang.python.antlr\n    60\tpipeline. It is the only Python frontend, installed on every host (JVM,\n    61\tNode and Dart each run their own generated target of the one grammar);\n    62\tthe hand-written Python compiler was retired at the frontend's migration\n    63\tgate. Select a frontend with the `lang` command by frontend id — a\n    64\tlanguage keyword is not a selector:\n    65\t\n    66\t```clojure\n    67\tyin> (lang :yang/python)\n    68\t\"Switched to :yang/python\"\n    69\tyin> print(\"hello\")\n    70\thello\n    71\t```\n    72\t\n    73\tSelection forms: only a qualified id names a frontend exactly\n    74\t(`(lang :yang/python)`), and `[id revision]` pins a revision; a language\n    75\tkeyword is not a selector — `(lang :python)` answers\n    76\t`Error: unavailable-parser: ...`. Selecting a frontend the catalog does\n    77\tnot hold answers the same error; there is no fallback to another\n    78\tfrontend.\n    79\t\n    80\tThe ANTLR frontend displays what the program prints, then the value of the\n    81\tsubmission's last top-level bare expression statement (`x = 1; x` shows\n    82\t`1`; an assignment, `print(...)` returning `None`, or a lone `None` shows\n    83\tnothing; the value is rendered as Python's `repr`). The display has three\n    84\trecorded edges: a bare expression inside a top-level compound statement\n    85\t(`if`/`for` bodies) does not display, where CPython's interactive mode\n    86\twould; a value captured before a later statement raises is printed after\n    87\tthe error text, where CPython prints it before the traceback; and string\n    88\t`repr` escapes only code units below 32 and 127, so a raw C1 control such\n    89\tas `'\\x80'` reaches the prompt unescaped. The frontend also keeps a frontend session across submissions (cleared with\n    90\tthe VM, as with `(reset)` and `(vm ...)`). The generated parser must exist\n    91\tbefore the REPL starts: `yin.repl.frontends` loads the parser namespace, so\n    92\ton a fresh clone run `bb gen:antlr` (or `clj -M:antlr-gen` for the JVM) once,\n    93\tor startup fails with class-not-found. The ANTLR frontend is the only Python\n    94\tfrontend; the catalog is `:yang/clojure` (default), `:yang/php`, and `:yang/python`,\n    95\teach on every host.\n    96\t\n    97\t## Saved state\n    98\t\n    99\tA node remembers how it was started. The flags it starts with are saved to\n   100\t`state.edn` in its node directory, `~/.yin/<name>` (`--name`, default `node`;\n   101\t`--dir` names the directory instead), and a bare `yin-repl` starts that node\n   102\tagain:\n   103\t\n   104\t```bash\n   105\tyin-repl dht serve --name b --listen 4002 --peer localhost:4001   # saved\n   106\tyin-repl --name b                                                  # resumes it\n   107\tyin-repl --name b --dht-peer 127.0.0.1:4003                        # changes the peer\n   108\t```\n   109\t\n   110\tThe banner says what happened: `state: saved to ...` on a first run,\n   111\t`state: resumed from ... (--index-store dht:..., ...)` on a restart, and\n   112\t`; the command line changed --dht-peer; saved` when flags overrode it. The\n   113\tfile is plain EDN and may be edited by hand:\n   114\t\n   115\t```clojure\n   116\t{:version 1\n   117\t :config {:index-store \"dht:/home/me/.yin/b\"\n   118\t          :dht-peer [\"127.0.0.1:4003\"]\n   119\t          :dht-port \"4002\"}}\n   120\t```\n   121\t\n   122\t- **What is saved:** `--index-store`, `--vm`, `--port`, `--headless`,\n   123\t  `--dht-peer`, `--dht-publish`, `--dht-bind`, `--dht-port`,\n   124\t  `--dht-max-inbound-bytes`, `--dht-key`, `--dht-principal` and\n   125\t  `--dht-follow`, which is what the `dht` subcommands expand to. The\n   126\t  one-shot `--dht-manifest` and `--dht-keygen` are never saved, and\n   127\t  neither is a followed head: that is `heads.edn`'s, in the store\n   128\t  directory (see Following a publisher's head). Definitions are not saved here: a durable\n   129\t  index store (`file:` or `dht:`) already keeps what was evaluated. The\n   130\t  evaluator chosen at the prompt with `(vm :type)` is not saved; start with\n   131\t  `--vm` to save one.\n   132\t- **Changing it:** a flag overrides the saved value for that setting, and the\n   133\t  result is saved. A repeated flag (`--dht-peer`, `--dht-principal`,\n   134\t  `--dht-follow`) replaces all of its saved values rather than adding to them.\n   135\t  `dht init` saves publishing and a key; `dht serve` and `dht join` clear both\n   136\t  from the saved state (a `--key` on that command line still stands). A bare\n   137\t  switch (`--headless`, `--dht-publish`) cannot be turned off by a flag, so\n   138\t  use `--reset`.\n   139\t- **`--reset`** forgets the saved state and starts from the command line\n   140\t  alone; the result is saved. **`--no-state`** neither reads nor writes it,\n   141\t  for a one-off run.\n   142\t- **Refusals:** a state file that cannot be read, or saved flags that clash\n   143\t  with the command line (saved `--dht-*` flags with `--index-store mem`), refuse\n   144\t  startup, naming the file and `--reset`. A state file that cannot be written\n   145\t  is a warning, and the node still starts.\n   146\t- **Hosts:** ClojureDart has no home directory, so a bare `yin-repl` there\n   147\t  saves nothing unless `--dir` names the node directory.\n   148\t- **One node per name:** the state file is only as protected as its\n   149\t  directory. `dht init|serve|join` put the store in the node directory, so the\n   150\t  store's lock also keeps one process per state file. A node whose store lives\n   151\t  elsewhere (`--index-store file:/tmp/a`, or the in-memory default) is not\n   152\t  covered: two such processes with the same `--name` or `--dir` overwrite each\n   153\t  other's file, last writer wins, and no process notices. Give each its own\n   154\t  node directory: a distinct `--name`, or a distinct `--dir` when you pass one.\n   155\t- **Values:** a flag's value that begins with `--` is taken as a missing\n   156\t  value and refused (`--dht-peer --dht-publish`), so a path that really\n   157\t  begins with `--` is written `./--name`.\n   158\t\n   159\t## Protocol and Architecture\n   160\t\n   161\t**`connect` returns immediately and reports its outcome when known.**\n   162\t`connect` composes the client boundary, attaches, and answers at once with\n   163\t`Attaching to …`; the driver prints `Connected to …` when the boundary\n   164\treports `/established`, and reports `/not-found` (an authoritative disclaimer,\n   165\tnot retried) or a reachability failure (which may succeed on retry) when that\n   166\tis what happened. No evaluation blocks on a promise, on any host.\n   167\t\n   168\t**`(vm :type)` offers four evaluators; the default is `:semantic`.**\n   169\tThe shell supports `:ast-walker` (the tree walker), `:semantic` (the linear\n   170\tsemantic VM), `:stack` (the de Bruijn stack kernel), and `:register` (the de\n   171\tBruijn register kernel). `:semantic` is the default when no `:vm-type` is\n   172\tgiven. Asking for any other type is an error naming what is supported.\n   173\tSwitching rebuilds the session and clears the value history.\n   174\t\n   175\tWhichever VM runs, the shell never reads a value from the VM record: a\n   176\thalted evaluation's value is appended to the output `dao.stream` medium as\n   177\ta `:repl/result` token, after the round's prints, and the shell reads it\n   178\tfrom there exactly as it reads printed output.\n   179\t\n   180\t**There is no `(telemetry)` command.** Telemetry is not part of this\n   181\tREPL architecture in any form. `(telemetry)` is answered with an unsupported\n   182\tnotice, and `--telemetry` / `--telemetry-stream` are rejected rather than\n   183\tignored. There is no `ws://` telemetry sink.\n   184\t\n   185\t**Evaluation runs on session-owned program media.** The REPL session\n   186\t(`make-session`), not any one VM, owns the program media for all four VMs.\n   187\tEvery program, including a datom program typed at the prompt, is appended\n   188\tto the session's ingress ring buffer (declared capacity 4096); the expander\n   189\tforwards it to the session's program medium, from which the selected VM\n   190\tingests it between batches. A gap (batches evicted before the VM saw them)\ntest/dao/space/published_read_test.cljc:71:      (is (= manifest-address (:manifest-address d)))\ntest/dao/space/published_read_test.cljc:75:      (is (= [manifest-address] @log) \"exactly the one manifest get\"))\ntest/dao/space/published_read_test.cljc:77:      (is (= {:manifest-address manifest-address}\ntest/dao/space/published_read_test.cljc:79:      (is (= manifest (index/read-manifest store manifest-address))))\ntest/dao/space/published_read_test.cljc:88:  (let [{:keys [store manifest-address]} (publish!)\ntest/dao/space/published_read_test.cljc:89:        pub (query/published-db store manifest-address)\ntest/dao/space/published_read_test.cljc:90:        coordinate (index/published-index {:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/mem} manifest-address)]\ntest/dao/space/published_read_test.cljc:96:  (let [{:keys [store manifest-address]} (publish!)\ntest/dao/space/published_read_test.cljc:97:        pub (query/published-db store manifest-address)\ntest/dao/space/published_read_test.cljc:114:            counted (query/published-db (counting store reads) manifest-address)\ntest/dao/space/published_read_test.cljc:124:            counted (query/published-db (counting store reads) manifest-address)\ntest/dao/space/published_read_test.cljc:139:  (let [{:keys [store manifest-address]} (publish!)\ntest/dao/space/published_read_test.cljc:140:        pub (query/published-db store manifest-address)\ntest/dao/space/published_read_test.cljc:167:      (let [{:keys [manifest manifest-address]} (publish! writer)\ntest/dao/space/published_read_test.cljc:169:                                              manifest-address)\ntest/dao/space/published_read_test.cljc:174:          (is (= #{:version :store :manifest-address :manifest} (set (keys d))))\ntest/dao/space/published_read_test.cljc:176:          (is (= manifest-address (:manifest-address d)))\ntest/dao/space/query_test.cljc:836:   {:store scratch-store :path path :manifest-address addr\ntest/dao/space/query_test.cljc:847:                {:keys [manifest-address]}\ntest/dao/space/query_test.cljc:859:                               manifest-address)]\ntest/dao/space/query_test.cljc:862:               :manifest-address manifest-address,\ntest/dao/space/query_test.cljc:1265:      (let [eager (index/read-datoms (:store fx) (:manifest-address fx))]\ntest/dao/space/query_test.cljc:1281:    (try (let [{:keys [manifest-address]} (index/publish-index! local [intake])\ntest/dao/space/query_test.cljc:1291:                                                 manifest-address)\ntest/dao/space/query_test.cljc:1295:                   :dao.stream/bound {:manifes